>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does a hot water cylinder cost in Liverpool?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A vented cylinder replaced starts from £750. An unvented cylinder starts from £1,450, and the price goes up with the size. All prices include VAT.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price, inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Vented cylinder replaced</td><td>from £750</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 150 litres</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 210 litres</td><td>from £1,695</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 250 to 300 litres</td><td>from £1,995</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Change from vented to unvented</td><td>from £1,895</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, vented cylinder</td><td>£175</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, unvented cylinder</td><td>£275</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder yearly service</td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Repairs and fault finding</td><td>£120 first hour (weekdays 9am to 4pm)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is in the price?</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new cylinder, supplied and fitted</li>
        <li>All pipe connections, valves and fittings</li>
        <li>Safety valves, expansion vessel and discharge pipe on unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>Testing and setting up before we leave</li>
        <li>Old cylinder drained, removed and taken away</li>
        <li>Building Regulations paperwork for unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Repairs out of hours are £150 for the first hour in the evening, at night and at weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. There is no call-out fee. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Vented or unvented: which do I need?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If you are happy with your shower and have a loft tank, a vented swap is the cheapest fix. If you want stronger showers or two bathrooms at once, go unvented.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Vented cylinder</th><th>Unvented cylinder</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Fed by</strong></td><td>A cold water tank in the loft</td><td>The mains water supply</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pressure</strong></td><td>Low, from gravity</td><td>Mains pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Showers</strong></td><td>Weaker, unless you add a pump</td><td>Strong at every tap</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Loft tank</strong></td><td>Needed</td><td>Not needed for hot water</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Who can fit it</strong></td><td>A qualified plumber</td><td>A G3 qualified engineer only</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Yearly service</strong></td><td>Not usually needed</td><td>Yes, to keep the warranty</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Price with us</strong></td><td>from £750</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Direct and indirect cylinders</h3>
      <p>An indirect cylinder is heated by your boiler. Hot water from the boiler runs through a coil inside the tank. This is the most common type in Liverpool homes. It usually has an immersion heater as a backup.</p>
      <p>A direct cylinder is heated by an immersion heater only. You find these in all-electric homes. We fit and replace both types.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="size">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What size hot water cylinder do I need?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on how many people live in your home and how many bathrooms you use at once. Too small and you run out. Too big and you heat water you never use.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Good for</th><th>Unvented price</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>150 litres</strong></td><td>1 to 2 people, one bathroom, flats and small homes</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>210 litres</strong></td><td>3 to 4 people, one bathroom, or a bath and a small en-suite</td><td>from £1,695</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>250 to 300 litres</strong></td><td>4 or more people, two bathrooms, lots of baths</td><td>from £1,995</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>This is a guide. Baths use far more hot water than showers. A big bath or a rain shower can push you up a size. We ask how you use hot water at the survey, then pick the size.</p>
      <p>We also check the space. Many Liverpool terraces have a small airing cupboard. A slim or shorter cylinder can often fit where a standard one will not.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="pressure">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Will an unvented cylinder work in my home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It will if your mains water supply is strong enough. We test your water pressure and flow at the survey, before we give you a price.</p>
      <p>An unvented cylinder can only give you what the mains gives it. Every maker sets a minimum pressure and flow. Some older Liverpool homes still have a narrow or old supply pipe. That can limit the flow, even when the pressure looks fine.</p>
      <p>If your supply is too weak, we tell you straight away. You will not pay for a cylinder that cannot do its job. We explain your options, such as keeping a vented system with a better cylinder.</p>
      <h3>An unvented cylinder also needs</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>A discharge pipe from the safety valves to a safe place outside</li>
        <li>Space for an expansion vessel</li>
        <li>A boiler that can heat it, such as a system or regular boiler</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Combi boiler or cylinder?</h3>
      <p>A combi boiler heats water as you use it. There is no tank, so it saves space. It suits flats and homes with one bathroom. See our <a href="/boiler-installation-liverpool">boiler installation prices</a>, from £2,195.</p>
      <p>A cylinder stores hot water, ready to use. It suits busy homes with two bathrooms, because two showers can run at once. If you want to swap a cylinder for a combi, our <a href="/central-heating-installation-liverpool">combi conversion</a> starts from £2,890.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Does my cylinder need repairing or replacing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Many faults can be fixed, such as a failed immersion heater or a faulty valve. If the tank itself is leaking, it needs replacing.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Often a repair</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>No hot water from the immersion heater</li>
            <li>Water is too hot or not hot enough</li>
            <li>Drip from a valve or fitting</li>
            <li>Banging or kettling noises from scale</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Usually a new cylinder</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>The tank itself is leaking or rusty</li>
            <li>You often run out of hot water</li>
            <li>Weak hot water, but strong cold water</li>
            <li>The cylinder is 20 years old or more</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Most cylinders last 15 to 25 years. An old one is more likely to split. A planned swap costs far less than repairing a ceiling after a burst.</p>
      <p>We find the cause first and tell you honestly if a repair will do. Repairs are charged from £120 for the first hour on weekdays.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>No hot water today?</strong> Call and we will tell you what we can do. Immersion heater swaps are often done the same day.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a cylinder installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We survey for free, give you a fixed written price, then fit the new cylinder. Most swaps are done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free survey.</strong> We check your space, your boiler and how much hot water you use. For unvented, we test your mains pressure and flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the cylinder, the fittings, the safety parts and the paperwork. No surprises on the day.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting day.</strong> We protect your floors, drain the old cylinder and take it out. Then we fit the new one and connect it up.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing.</strong> We fill the system, check for leaks and test every safety valve. We set the temperature and show you the controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Paperwork.</strong> Unvented work is notified as the Building Regulations require. You get the certificate and the maker's warranty details to keep.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Why G3 matters</h3>
      <p>An unvented cylinder holds hot water under mains pressure. Its safety valves must work every time. That is why the Building Regulations say only a qualified engineer can fit one. All our engineers are G3 qualified. Never let anyone fit an unvented cylinder without it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="service">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do unvented cylinders need servicing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes, once a year. Makers ask for it to keep the warranty valid, and it checks the safety parts still work. Our unvented cylinder service is £120 including VAT.</p>
      <h3>What the service includes</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Test the temperature and pressure relief valve</li>
        <li>Check and top up the expansion vessel pressure</li>
        <li>Check the tundish and discharge pipe are clear</li>
        <li>Check the pressure reducing valve and inlet strainer</li>
        <li>Check the thermostats and immersion heater</li>
        <li>Fill in the service record for your warranty</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Book it with your <a href="/boiler-service-liverpool">boiler service</a> to save a second visit. We send you a reminder next year.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="brands">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which cylinder brands do you fit?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We fit leading UK brands, including Megaflo, Gledhill, Telford, Joule and Ariston. We pick the one that suits your space, your boiler and your budget.</p>
      <h3>Special cylinders</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Heat pump cylinders.</strong> These have a bigger coil to work with the lower water temperature from a heat pump. We supply and fit them, ready for your heat pump installer.</li>
        <li><strong>Solar cylinders.</strong> A twin coil cylinder, with one coil for the boiler and one for solar panels.</li>
        <li><strong>Slim and horizontal cylinders.</strong> For small cupboards, low ceilings and eaves.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Jobs we do most often in Liverpool</h3>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Loft tank to mains pressure</h3>
          <p>A Victorian terrace with an old copper cylinder and a loft tank. We fit an unvented cylinder. The showers get stronger, and the loft tank is no longer needed for hot water.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Combi struggling with two bathrooms</h3>
          <p>A combi that cannot keep up with a family bathroom and an en-suite. A system boiler with an unvented cylinder lets both run at once.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
